WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.627
Domain 2 primer: Tool Design & MCP Integration.

2
00:00:03.627 --> 00:00:12.276
Agents act through tools, so how you name, describe, scope and connect those tools decides whether the agent does the right thing.

3
00:00:13.676 --> 00:00:15.727
Welcome to tool design and MCP.

4
00:00:15.727 --> 00:00:21.749
A tool is a function that Claude can ask your application to run, like looking up an order.

5
00:00:21.749 --> 00:00:23.602
Claude never sees your code.

6
00:00:23.602 --> 00:00:28.102
It sees the tool's name, a description, and a schema for its inputs.

7
00:00:28.102 --> 00:00:34.455
MCP, the Model Context Protocol, is a standard way to connect servers full of tools to an agent.

8
00:00:35.855 --> 00:00:37.798
Here is the loop in plain terms.

9
00:00:37.798 --> 00:00:39.741
You give Claude a list of tools.

10
00:00:39.741 --> 00:00:44.721
Claude reads the descriptions and decides which one to call, and with what inputs.

11
00:00:44.721 --> 00:00:49.215
Your code runs it and sends back a result, or an error marked as an error.

12
00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:52.008
Claude reads that and decides what to do next.

13
00:00:52.008 --> 00:00:56.441
A setting called tool choice controls whether calling a tool is optional.

14
00:00:57.841 --> 00:01:00.916
The five task statements follow a natural order.

15
00:01:00.916 --> 00:01:04.695
First, describe tools clearly so the right one gets picked.

16
00:01:04.695 --> 00:01:07.898
Second, return errors that help the agent recover.

17
00:01:07.898 --> 00:01:12.190
Third, distribute tools so each agent has a small, role-shaped set.

18
00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.738
Fourth, connect MCP servers with the right scope and no leaked secrets.

19
00:01:16.738 --> 00:01:22.119
Fifth, use Claude Code's own built-in tools for reading, editing and searching code.

20
00:01:23.519 --> 00:01:25.824
Here are the words this domain uses most.

21
00:01:25.824 --> 00:01:28.916
Pause the video and read through them, then keep going.

22
00:01:30.316 --> 00:01:32.492
Carry four ideas into the lessons.

23
00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:38.124
Descriptions steer which tool gets picked, so improve them before adding routing layers.

24
00:01:38.124 --> 00:01:41.708
Errors are messages to the agent, so make them specific.

25
00:01:41.708 --> 00:01:44.972
Smaller tool sets per agent produce better choices.

26
00:01:44.972 --> 00:01:51.244
And shared configuration lives in the repository, while secrets live in each person's environment.

